# 8. Threats to validity, limitations and future work

**Draft.** Numbers from the files named in `data/frame-numbers.json`.

## 8.1 Construct validity: what "correct" means here

A prediction is counted as correct when it equals the BRAM_18K total of Vitis' C-synthesis report
(`csynth.xml`), and per array when it equals the memory table, which can be scored only where the two
tools' memory names align ({{ev_pa_aligned_pct}} % of the BRAM-carrying array rows, Table 6.3). This is Vitis' own *estimate* after C
synthesis, not the BRAM of the implemented design after Vivado place and route, which can differ (Vivado
may merge or trim memories). hls-est therefore reproduces Vitis' decisions, which is the stated goal, and
inherits any distance between Vitis' report and the final bitstream. For {{ev_over_device_compared}} of the
{{compared}} compared designs ({{ev_over_device_compared_pct}} %), Vitis allocates more than the
{{ev_device_capacity}} BRAM_18K of the part. These designs can never be implemented on it, so Vitis's
numbers for them are never checked by an implementation run. On the {{ev_fits_compared}} designs that fit,
{{ev_fits_exact}} are exact and {{ev_fits_in_band}} in band (Table 6.1).

The goal band (exact or at most {{band_over_pct}} % over) is asymmetric by design: {{under}} of the {{compared}}
compared designs under-count and are out of band whatever their size, and {{over_gt_band}} over-count by more
than {{band_over_pct}} %. Measured in BRAM_18K, the under-counts sum to {{under_sum}} and the over-counts to {{over_sum}}.
Table 8.1 (`tables/frame-undercounts.md`) lists the largest under-counts with their largest per-array
differences.

## 8.2 Internal validity: one tool, one part, one configuration

- **One version, one part, one clock, one partitioning mode.** Every ground-truth run uses Vitis
  {{vitis_versions}} on {{vitis_parts}} at {{vitis_clocks}} with `throughput_driven=off`. Thresholds such
  as the LUT-RAM limit (N5), the depth bands (N8) and the 17-slot port model of replication (N4) are
  measured for this device family; the configuration file makes them data, but their values for other
  parts and other versions are not measured. With `throughput_driven=auto` Vitis partitions by port
  pressure (N2); a second, informational run set exists, but hls-est's model of it is behind an option and
  is not evaluated here (O2).
- **The catalogue was also the development set.** The rules were found by chasing mismatches on the same
  catalogue that is used to report accuracy. Each rule was proven on separate minimal kernels and the
  project's rules forbid branching on benchmark names, array names or sizes, but no held-out set of designs
  was kept back. The kernels themselves were mostly written to explain a catalogue mismatch, so they are
  not independent of the catalogue either. The reported exact share is therefore an in-sample number;
  Section 8.6 proposes a held-out evaluation.
- **Repeatability of the ground truth.** No Vitis run was repeated on purpose. {{ev_rep_groups}} groups of
  catalogue designs with byte-identical cgeist MLIR ({{ev_rep_designs}} designs) were synthesised as
  separate Vitis runs, and their totals agree in {{ev_rep_totals_agree}} groups and their memory tables in
  {{ev_rep_memtables_agree}}. This is indirect evidence only; a deliberate repeat of a sample of runs,
  including runs on a second host, is open.
- **No independent baseline from the literature.** The only baseline is a naive declared-size model
  (Table 6.4: exact on {{ev_b0_exact}} of {{compared}} designs). The learned predictors of Chapter 2 predict
  other targets (post-implementation resources, aggregates) and were not run on this catalogue.
- **Ground-truth gaps.** {{vitis_failed}} catalogue designs fail in Vitis and {{vitis_timeout}} time out;
  among the failures, {{dod_vitis_rejects}} are sources Vitis itself rejects. They have no ground truth and are excluded from
  every accuracy number ({{ev_all_no_vitis}} of the {{designs}} designs have no Vitis result;
  {{ev_all_estimated_no_vitis}} of them are estimated by hls-est).
- **Front-end correctness.** An estimate is only as good as the MLIR it starts from. Differential
  execution checks {{sem_checked}} designs ({{sem_equal}} bit-exact, {{sem_mismatch}} mismatching);
  {{sem_na}} designs cannot be checked, most because their native reference build is defective.

## 8.3 External validity: an academic corpus

The HLSFactory catalogue consists of published benchmark suites, course projects and generated designs.
It contains many small kernels and many near-duplicates (PolyBench in several sizes and number types; the
`WIP` dataset repeats the `stream_hls` designs), so design counts overstate diversity:
{{datasets_all_exact}} of the {{datasets}} datasets are exact on every compared design, and four datasets
carry {{err_top4_share}} % of the summed absolute error ({{err_top4}} BRAM_18K). Industrial designs (deep
dataflow hierarchies, many `m_axi` bundles, vendor libraries such as the Vitis vision or DSP libraries,
`hls::task`) are under-represented; a few catalogue designs need library headers the Vitis
{{vitis_versions}} install no longer ships (`hls_video.h`) and have no ground truth.

## 8.4 Limitations of the model

- **Under-count classes.** The last round of attribution (O113) groups the out-of-band designs by cause
  per array. The largest open classes are: loops hls-est leaves unscheduled because one operation in them
  has no timing, most often `hls::stream` reads and writes (O54 (c), O111; N82 implements the Vitis rule
  but stays opt-in because it needs O1, see O114); the greedy modulo scheduler returning a larger II than
  Vitis on large flattened bodies (O56); flatten refusals Vitis makes that hls-est does not (N64, N76);
  and `hls::vector` and struct cases (O54, O64). Rule interactions are tested mainly by catalogue designs:
  {{ev_inter_two_or_more}} of the {{compared}} compared designs change under two or more of the rule pairs
  of Table 6.6 (an upper bound; {{ev_inter_ctl_two_or_more}} under two or more controlled pairs).
- **O1: loop-carried memory dependences.** Recurrences through memory (`a[i] = f(a[i-1])`) never reach
  hls-est's modulo schedulers, a defect inherited from the upstream pass hls-est's scheduler is built on:
  only recurrences through SSA loop-carried values are honoured. Activating the dependences broke other
  schedules in an experiment (infeasible problems, multiple sinks) and was reverted. A loop that Vitis
  slows down to a higher II therefore looks faster to hls-est, which over-states its port demand and can
  change the storage kind and replication; the RTL back end has to raise the II after the fact (Chapter 7).
- **Rule inference may be incomplete.** {{n_notes_unconfirmed}} notes are explicitly unconfirmed and
  {{n_notes_partly}} only partly confirmed. A rule that holds on every kernel tried can still fail on a
  combination nobody tried; kernels sweep one parameter at a time and interactions of rules are covered
  only where a catalogue design exercised them. Several current rules were found by a later counterexample
  to an earlier, simpler rule (for example N38 corrects the inputs of N4's copy count; N76 qualifies N64),
  which suggests more such refinements remain.
- **Only BRAM.** DSP, FF and LUT are not estimated. A first feasibility check (MEMOS #37) found that they
  depend on Vitis' schedule and operator binding, which hls-est models only for memory ports.
- **Run time.** The median estimate takes {{ev_time_hlsest_median_s2}} s, but a few large designs need
  minutes in the dependence analysis and scheduler (O103, O105). In the final run, {{ev_all_hlsest_fail}}
  compiled designs end without an estimate: {{ev_all_hlsest_timeout}} exceed the harness's 120 s limit and
  in {{ev_all_top_not_found}} hls-est finds no function with the top's name. Only
  {{ev_all_hlsest_fail_with_vitis}} of them have a Vitis result.

## 8.5 Legality of the method

The method is behavioural black-box inference plus the use of AMD's open-sourced front end:

- Vitis is run as a user runs it, with documented options and hooks, and only its documented outputs are
  read: reports, logs, the intermediate `.ll` files it writes into its project directory, the generated
  RTL, and its own `-help` / `-help-hidden` text.
- The open-source front end `hls-llvm-project` is read, and code from it is ported only from files whose
  licence allows it (the Apache-2.0 Xilinx HLS files and the NCSA-licensed LLVM base, with their notices;
  MEMOS #40). Its `XilinxPlat` files carry no licence grant and are read but never copied; the Vitis
  headers are compiled, never copied.
- The installed device database `platform.db` is read read-only for device facts (MEMOS #42); it and dumps
  of it are never copied into the repositories.
- No closed Vitis binary or library is disassembled, decompiled or patched.

Whether the Vitis end-user licence agreement restricts the publication of measurements or comparisons
made with the tool has **not** been checked as part of this work; it should be checked (with the
supervisor and, if needed, the university's legal office) before publication. This is a question about the
publication of results, separate from the legality of the inference method itself.

## 8.6 Future work

- **DSP, FF and LUT (Phase 6).** Extend the cost model to the other resource classes. It needs Vitis'
  schedule and operator binding, so the scheduling plugin interfaces (operator timing, sequential
  scheduler, binder) that are placeholders today must become models; `hls-doc/design/phase6-plan.md`
  holds a plan and the first counting rules (N34).
- **O1 and the stream timing.** Feed loop-carried memory dependences to both schedulers with the
  forwarded latency Vitis uses, then enable the hls::stream timing (N82, O114).
- **A second part and the other partitioning mode.** Repeat the ground truth on an UltraScale+ or Versal
  part and with `throughput_driven=auto`, measure which thresholds move, and express them as a second
  device and tool profile. This is the first real test of the configuration and plugin architecture beyond
  the unvalidated PolarFire example.
- **Repeat the controlled measurements.** Re-run the pass ablation (Table 6.7, measured in round 5 under the
  exactclass shim) and the historical rule pairs of Table 6.6 as controlled one-key-off runs of the final
  pipeline, and repeat a sample of Vitis runs to bound the ground truth's repeatability.
- **Held-out evaluation.** Freeze hls-est and evaluate it on designs that played no part in its
  development (a later HLSFactory release, industrial examples, or a newer Vitis version on the same
  designs) to obtain an out-of-sample accuracy.
- **RTL coverage.** Implement the most frequent blockers of the RTL back end (dynamic shapes, streams and
  dataflow, kept calls, pointer and struct code, global arrays), pipelined floating-point units for timing
  closure, and a comparison of the generated RTL's area and timing with Vitis' (Chapter 7).
- **Automating the inference.** The hypothesis-kernel-sweep loop is manual. Kernel generators that sweep
  thresholds automatically, and active-learning methods from model learning [@vaandrager2017model], could
  shorten it and make the coverage of rule interactions measurable.
